<rml>
<head>
	<title>Generic Menu Dialog</title>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="base.rcss"/>
	<link type="text/rcss" href="generic_menu_dialog.rcss"/>
</head>
<body>
	<!-- Sibling primitive to generic_confirm_dialog.rml -- see GenericMenuDialog.h's own class
	     comment for why this is a separate primitive (arbitrary N-button list vs two fixed named
	     buttons) and why it's a single document, unlike generic_confirm_dialog's fg/bg split (no
	     item3D here, so no compositing-order problem to solve).

	     This is legacy's own markup in practice -- modern has its own complete fork
	     (themes/modern/generic_menu_dialog.rml). Keep ids/data-model/event names in sync,
	     GenericMenuDialog.cpp binds both by name. -->
	<div id="panel" class="center-both" data-model="generic_menu_dialog">
		<!-- Native always draws IMAGE_MSGBOX_BACK first, underneath the top/middle/bottom caps
		     below (those are just a frame with a transparent center) -- same asset/mechanism as
		     generic_confirm_dialog_bg.rcss's own .gcd-bg-sprite-back. Without this the panel
		     shows the frame art but nothing behind it. -->
		<div class="gmd-sprite-back"></div>
		<div class="gmd-sprite-top"></div>
		<div class="gmd-sprite-middle"></div>
		<div class="gmd-sprite-bottom"></div>
		<!-- Native's own IMAGE_MSGBOX_TOP_TITLEBAR sprite swap for an optional title -- same
		     technique generic_confirm_dialog.rcss already proves; painted after (on top of)
		     .gmd-sprite-top above. -->
		<div class="gmd-header-rail" data-class-hidden="!has_title"></div>
		<div class="gmd-header-title" data-class-hidden="!has_title">{{title}}</div>
		<div class="gmd-body" data-class-has-title="has_title">
			<div class="gmd-line" data-for="line : lines" data-class-bold="line.bold">{{line.text}}</div>
			<!-- No explicit column count -- .gmd-button-row's own flex-wrap lets the button
			     count/width and the row's available space determine layout, same technique the
			     numeric-keypad grid in generic_confirm_dialog.rcss already proves. Every native
			     consumer's own fixed 128dp button width only ever fits one per row here, so this
			     naturally reproduces their single-column stack with no config knob needed. -->
			<div class="gmd-button-row">
				<!-- it_index (RmlUi's own data-for loop variable) passed straight into the click
				     handler -- proven pattern, see char_make.rml's job-select buttons for the
				     original precedent. gmd-button-cell wraps each button together with its own
				     optional description lines (button.lines, a nested data-for -- RmlUi
				     re-parses each outer iteration's own inner RML, so the inner loop and the
				     outer button/it_index aliases both resolve normally) so text that describes
				     one specific button (e.g. CChaosMixMenuMsgBox's per-recipe blurb) renders
				     directly above that button instead of bunched into the shared summary above. -->
				<div class="gmd-button-cell" data-for="button : buttons" data-class-compact="button.compact" data-class-has-lines="button.has_lines">
					<div class="gmd-line" data-for="line : button.lines" data-class-bold="line.bold">{{line.text}}</div>
					<div class="btn gmd-btn" data-class-compact="button.compact" data-class-disabled="!button.enabled"
					     data-event-click="gmd_button_click(it_index)">
						{{button.label}}
						<span class="gmd-tooltip" data-class-hidden="!button.has_tooltip">{{button.tooltip}}</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
